>
  <p>Most students read a textbook the way they read a novel: start at the top, move steadily to the bottom, highlight the bits that look important. An hour later the page is yellow and the memory is empty. Textbooks are reference tools, and they reward a very different kind of reading.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why passive reading fails</h2>
  <p>Dunlosky's review rated highlighting and re-reading as low-utility techniques. They create a comforting sense of familiarity without building the ability to recall. The fix is to make reading active, forcing your brain to retrieve and answer rather than simply recognise the words again.</p>

  <h2 id="sq3r">The SQ3R method</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Survey.</strong> Skim the headings, summary and any questions to get the shape of the section first.</li>
    <li><strong>Question.</strong> Turn each heading into a question. "Photosynthesis" becomes "how does photosynthesis work?".</li>
    <li><strong>Read.</strong> Read one section at a time, hunting for the answer to your question.</li>
    <li><strong>Recite.</strong> Look away and answer from memory, in your own words. McDaniel's read-recite-review studies show this step is what makes it stick.</li>
    <li><strong>Review.</strong> Later, go back over your questions and answers to consolidate.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="notes">What to write down</h2>
  <p>Write answers to your heading-questions in your own words, not sentences copied from the book. Keep it brief: a key idea, how it connects, one example. These notes double as recall questions for later revision. For the different note layouts, see <a href="/guides/how-to-take-better-notes/">how to take better notes</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="apply">Fit it into study</h2>
  <p>SQ3R is slower per page and far faster per unit of learning, so budget realistic time for it: plan reading blocks with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> and run each section in a focused <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro</a>. Because the recite step is retrieval practice, this pairs naturally with <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">active recall and spaced repetition</a>.</p>
</Guide>
